@inherits LayoutComponentBase

<div class="demo-banner" role="note">
    <strong>In-browser demo.</strong> Everything here — forms, drafts, and submissions — lives
    only in this tab's memory. Refreshing the page loses it.
</div>

@* A real toggle button, not a decorative link. aria-pressed is the ONE state signal -- the
   button's own accessible name ("High contrast") never changes, so assistive technology
   announces exactly "High contrast, pressed" / "not pressed" on activation, rather than that
   PLUS a redundant textual "On"/"Off" suffix double-announcing the same state. The sighted
   signal is a CSS treatment keyed off aria-pressed itself (MainLayout.razor.css), not separate
   text. _isHighContrast (MainLayout.razor.cs) is the single source of truth Blazor re-renders
   this attribute from; JS interop (MainLayout.razor.js) is used for nothing but the one DOM
   mutation outside Blazor's own component tree -- data-bf-theme lives on <html> itself
   (docs/accessibility-statement-plan.md, Increment C4). In normal document flow, not a fixed
   overlay -- see MainLayout.razor.css's own remarks on WCAG 2.4.11. *@
<div class="demo-toolbar">
    <button type="button"
            id="demo-theme-toggle"
            class="demo-theme-toggle"
            aria-pressed="@(_isHighContrast ? "true" : "false")"
            @onclick="ToggleHighContrastAsync">
        High contrast
    </button>
</div>

<main class="bf-page">
    @Body
</main>

@* #blazor-error-ui already lives in wwwroot/index.html, outside the app's own root -- Blazor
   overwrites #app's contents on a fatal error, so the div showing the error bar has to sit
   outside the component tree to survive that, matching every default Blazor template. A second
   copy here would only sit unstyled (CSS isolation never reaches into index.html's markup). *@
